{
  "data": {
    "A": "Research ID lookup acceleration in src/graph.c. Replace find_by_id's repeated linear scans with an index built once after reading input; start with a compact open-addressed hash table supporting arbitrary integer IDs. Preserve missing-ID behavior and first-record lookup semantics. Do not assume IDs are dense, ordered, or nonnegative. Initially retain the existing traversal and neighbor parsing to isolate the lookup benefit. Compare index construction cost against saved searches using unchanged automatic measurements; small graphs may favor a simple fallback. Deliver a correct standalone candidate and its measured results.",
    "B": "Research demand-driven memoization and stack-safe evaluation. Each node has one selected successor, determined by the first strictly largest positive immediate neighbor value—not by accumulated path cost. Cache successor decisions and completed float costs across queries. Replace recursive evaluation with an explicit path stack, then unwind toward the root, computing each parent value plus its completed child cost. Preserve that addition order and float rounding; a forward running sum is not equivalent. Terminal nodes must retain their value without adding an artificial zero. Preserve missing-ID returns and the reference's -1 successor sentinel. Prefer evaluating only queried chains over eagerly processing the entire DAG. Keep lookup changes separable for integration with A.",
    "C": "Research low-overhead input and representation improvements. Replace repeated formatted input and string-token copying with buffered, line-aware reading and compact integer neighbor storage, while preserving valid-input semantics and original query-token spelling. Use libc float conversion or a demonstrated equivalent; do not introduce approximate decimal parsing. Separate parsing improvements from traversal changes so their benefit can be measured independently. Compare eager neighbor decoding with deferred decoding when few nodes are visited. Preserve query order and exact '%s: %f\\n' output formatting. Prioritize serial efficiency: at the measured runtime, thread startup and synchronization may outweigh four-core parallelism.",
    "G": "Integrate only programs produced by A/B/C, retaining the best measured standalone candidate as a fallback. Prioritize combining indexed lookup with demand-driven memoization, then assess C's parsing/storage changes independently, within the existing budget. Reconcile ownership and lifetimes of the ID index, neighbor storage, query tokens, and cached costs. Audit missing references, strict-positive selection, stable ties, sentinel handling, terminal values, and bottom-up float arithmetic. Run the unchanged correctness and timing machinery for each retained integration. Do not infer additive gains: preprocessing and allocation costs can make a combination slower. Select the best measured correct program, not necessarily the most comprehensive merge. Leave tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions, and budgets unchanged.",
    "analysis": "The supplied implementation is Problem F, Graph Search on a DAG. It greedily follows the neighbor with the greatest immediate value exceeding zero; it does not maximize total path weight. Equal values retain the first neighbor, absent IDs contribute zero, and results use recursively grouped float additions. Repeated linear ID searches, neighbor-string parsing, and recomputation of shared suffixes are plausible optimization targets, but no profile or input dimensions were supplied. This run's baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 0.0115714 seconds across three samples (0.0115307–0.0116204 seconds). That short total runtime makes preprocessing and parallel overhead important. The three research trees therefore separate lookup, evaluation reuse, and parsing improvements. The ARM64 target has four CPU cores and no GPU; no hardware-specific speedup is assumed."
